@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Single app server hosting one artifact, plus a database.
'   usecase:      Smallest teaching deployment — one app node + one DB node.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       deployment
' ============================================================================
title Example Deployment Diagram - Simple Single Server

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Deployment diagrams show how SOFTWARE ARTIFACTS (binaries, jars, WAR files)
' are deployed onto HARDWARE NODES (servers, devices, VMs) and how those
' nodes talk to each other. Key vocabulary:
'
'   node          -> a physical or virtual machine, drawn as a 3D box
'   artifact      -> a deployable software package that lives INSIDE a node
'   database      -> a specialized node shape for data stores
'   -->           -> a communication path (association) between two nodes
'
' Stereotypes refine what kind of node/artifact you mean:
'   <<device>>               a piece of physical hardware (a real box)
'   <<executionEnvironment>> a software runtime that hosts other software
'                           (an OS, a JVM, a container runtime)
'   <<deploy>>              an artifact deployed onto a node
'   <<database>>            a data store (also expressible via the
'                           `database` keyword for the shape)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' A single application server — modeled as a <<device>> (a real/virtual box).
' `node` renders as a 3D cube. Add a stereotype to say WHAT KIND of node.
node "App Server" as app <<device>> {
  ' Artifacts live INSIDE the node body. Use `artifact` + stereotype `<<deploy>>`
  ' to show "this software package is deployed here."
  artifact "UML-IDE Backend\n(web-1.0.0.jar)" as backend <<deploy>>
}

' A database node — the `database` keyword gives the classic cylinder shape.
database "PostgreSQL DB" as db <<database>> {
  ' Databases host artifacts too: the schema/seed data is a deployable thing.
  artifact "UML-IDE Schema\n(schema-1.0.0.sql)" as schema <<deploy>>
}

' Communication path between the two nodes.
' `-->` draws a solid arrowhead line; the `: JDBC` suffix labels the protocol.
' Use a label to show HOW they talk (JDBC, HTTPS, gRPC, TCP, etc.).
app --> db : JDBC

' A short teaching note pinned to the diagram. Notes are great for
' calling out WHY a relationship exists or flagging assumptions.
note right of db
  The app connects to the DB
  over the standard JDBC port (5432).
end note

' Recap (read this in the source — it won't render):
'   * TWO nodes total: one `node` (3D box) + one `database` (cylinder).
'   * Each node HOSTS an artifact via the <<deploy>> stereotype.
'   * One communication path, labeled with its protocol.
' That is the minimum vocabulary for ANY deployment diagram.

@enduml
